    {
      "id": "UP-50aef09fe369cbd6",
      "kind": "prose",
      "local_module": "m67044",
      "upstream_module": "m42240",
      "source_id": "import-auto-id1426952",
      "status": "unreviewed-difference",
      "local": "The spring constant [MATH] is related to the rigidity (or stiffness) of a system—the larger the spring constant, the greater the restoring force, and the stiffer the system. The units of [MATH] are newtons per meter (N/m). shows a graph of the absolute value of the restoring force versus the displacement for a system that can be described by Hooke’s law—a simple spring in this case. The slope of the graph equals the spring constant [MATH] in newtons per meter.",
      "upstream": "The force constant [MATH] is related to the rigidity (or stiffness) of a system—the larger the force constant, the greater the restoring force, and the stiffer the system. The units of [MATH] are newtons per meter (N/m). For example, [MATH] is directly related to Young’s modulus when we stretch a string. shows a graph of the absolute value of the restoring force versus the displacement for a system that can be described by Hooke’s law—a simple spring in this case. The slope of the graph equals the force constant [MATH] in newtons per meter. A common physics laboratory exercise is to measure restoring forces created by springs, determine if they follow Hooke’s law, and calculate their force constants if they do.",
      "local_html": "The spring constant <em><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow /></mrow></mrow></math></em> is related to the rigidity (or stiffness) of a system—the larger the spring constant, the greater the restoring force, and the stiffer the system. The units of <em><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow /></mrow></mrow></math></em> are newtons per meter (N/m). <span class=\"muted\">[reference]</span> shows a graph of the absolute value of the restoring force versus the displacement for a system that can be described by Hooke’s law—a simple spring in this case. The slope of the graph equals the spring constant <em><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow /></mrow></mrow></math></em> in newtons per meter.",
      "upstream_html": "The force constant <em><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></math></em> is related to the rigidity (or stiffness) of a system—the larger the force constant, the greater the restoring force, and the stiffer the system. The units of <em><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></math></em> are newtons per meter (N/m). For example, <em><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></math></em> is directly related to Young’s modulus when we stretch a string. <span class=\"muted\">[reference]</span> shows a graph of the absolute value of the restoring force versus the displacement for a system that can be described by Hooke’s law—a simple spring in this case. The slope of the graph equals the force constant <em><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></math></em> in newtons per meter. A common physics laboratory exercise is to measure restoring forces created by springs, determine if they follow Hooke’s law, and calculate their force constants if they do.",
      "exercise_context": false,
      "credit_review_required": false
    },
