    {
      "id": "UP-50f8ae85a23be933",
      "kind": "prose",
      "local_module": "m67044",
      "upstream_module": "m42240",
      "source_id": "import-auto-id2612856",
      "status": "unreviewed-difference",
      "local": "Consider the car to be in its equilibrium position [MATH] before the person gets in. The car then settles down 1.20 cm, which means it is displaced to a position [MATH] . At that point, the springs supply a restoring force [MATH] equal to the person’s weight [MATH] . We take this force to be [MATH] in Hooke’s law. Knowing [MATH] and [MATH] , we can then solve the spring constant [MATH] .",
      "upstream": "Consider the car to be in its equilibrium position [MATH] before the person gets in. The car then settles down 1.20 cm, which means it is displaced to a position [MATH] . At that point, the springs supply a restoring force [MATH] equal to the person’s weight [MATH] . We take this force to be [MATH] in Hooke’s law. Knowing [MATH] and [MATH] , we can then solve the force constant [MATH] .",
      "local_html": "Consider the car to be in its equilibrium position <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mrow /></mrow></mrow></math> before the person gets in. The car then settles down 1.20 cm, which means it is displaced to a position <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mtext>.</mtext><mrow><mtext>20</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">×</mo><msup><mtext>10</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mspace width=\"0.25em\" /><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow><mrow /></mrow></mrow></math>. At that point, the springs supply a restoring force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi></mrow><mrow /></mrow></mrow></math> equal to the person’s weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">=</mo><mstyle fontstyle=\"italic\" style=\";font-style:italic\"><mrow><mtext>mg</mtext></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mrow><mtext>80</mtext><mtext>.</mtext><mn>0</mn><mi /><mspace /><mspace width=\"0.25em\" /><mtext> kg</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mrow><mn>9</mn><mtext>.</mtext><mtext>80</mtext><mi /><mspace width=\"0.25em\" /><msup><mtext>m/s</mtext><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">=</mo><mtext>784</mtext></mrow><mi /><mspace width=\"0.25em\" /><mtext>N</mtext></mrow></mrow><mrow /></mrow></mrow></math>. We take this force to be <em><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi></mrow><mrow /></mrow></mrow></math></em> in Hooke’s law. Knowing <em><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi></mrow><mrow /></mrow></mrow></math></em> and <em><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow><mrow /></mrow></mrow></math></em>, we can then solve the spring constant <em><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow /></mrow></mrow></math></em>. ",
      "upstream_html": "Consider the car to be in its equilibrium position <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow></math> before the person gets in. The car then settles down 1.20 cm, which means it is displaced to a position <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mtext>.</mtext><mrow><mtext>20</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">×</mo><msup><mtext>10</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mspace width=\"0.25em\" /><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></math>. At that point, the springs supply a restoring force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi></mrow></mrow></math> equal to the person’s weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">=</mo><mstyle fontstyle=\"italic\" style=\";font-style:italic\"><mrow><mtext>mg</mtext></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mrow><mtext>80</mtext><mtext>.</mtext><mn>0</mn><mi /><mspace /><mspace width=\"0.25em\" /><mtext> kg</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mrow><mn>9</mn><mtext>.</mtext><mtext>80</mtext><mi /><mspace width=\"0.25em\" /><msup><mtext>m/s</mtext><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">=</mo><mtext>784</mtext></mrow><mi /><mspace width=\"0.25em\" /><mtext>N</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></math>. We take this force to be <em><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi></mrow></mrow></math></em> in Hooke’s law. Knowing <em><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi></mrow></mrow></math></em> and <em><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></math></em>, we can then solve the force constant <em><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></math></em>. ",
